<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
  <p class="font-bold">
    An extra metre of string raises the loop by about 16 centimetres everywhere.
  </p>
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 300 290"
    class="w-full max-w-sm"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="girdle-title girdle-desc"
  >
    <title id="girdle-title">A circular Earth with its string raised by a uniform gap</title>
    <desc id="girdle-desc">
      An orange outer circle surrounds the Earth. The diagram exaggerates the tiny 16 centimetre
      gap. The increase in circumference is one metre.
    </desc>
    <circle
      cx="150"
      cy="118"
      r="78"
      fill="#387a91"
      fill-opacity=".25"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="2"
    />
    <circle cx="150" cy="118" r="96" fill="none" stroke="#bd4b37" stroke-width="3" />
    <path d="M150 22 V40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
    <path d="M18 252 H42" stroke="#bd4b37" stroke-width="3" />
    <path d="M30 266 V282" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="16">
      <text x="50" y="257">
        Extra metre of string
      </text>
      <text x="50" y="278">
        Gap at every point: 0.159 m
      </text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <div class="text-base font-bold">Extra radius = 1 m ÷ (2π) ≈ 0.159 m</div>
  <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
    Circumference is 2π times radius. Add 1 m to any circle’s circumference and its radius grows
    by 1/(2π) m, no matter how large the circle was. The gap here is hugely exaggerated so it
    can be seen.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
